{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 07b454ee2d6c061392f5f2cd6cfd72f968fc9f62, against round-16 report d7b948f481dde940a5b42bbad9f0bc28b8a105cf. Worktree clean, make vet and make test pass, whitespace clean.\n\nFinding 1: the profile anchor was a name, and I read the fold before writing this time. Measured, the gap was total -- the workroom's ratifiable kinds are assert, propose, roster, infra-key and seal, an admission-profile statement records as an opaque kind, and its ratification is refused 'statement kind is not ratifiable'. So the act the note described could not happen. What replaces it is a contract marked as design throughout: one governance kind admission-profile carrying bundle, contract and genesis; three fold registrations and no new judgement; the ratifier authority that already exists. Selection is a read over a prefix, resolvable because the fold is already a pure function of a log prefix -- measured by folding constructed prefixes, including the case where superseding the newer activation returns the older one to force.\n\nMy first bootstrap profile was wrong in this note's own recurring way. I wrote that it denotes the kernel's own admission and needs no interpreter, which is an unversioned label for code that changes -- the exact hazard the profile pin exists to remove. It now takes its contract version from the genesis descriptor, gitseq-genesis-v0: measured, that is the kernel's literal genesis marker and the descriptor version field is validated to be exactly 0. So it takes no exemption and profile-unavailable is reachable for it in principle. Its object is measured against git commit-tree at 6ad9570a5b4f824304c855a25511001e126f6a3c.\n\nFinding 2: keyat(H) is the key in force for commits appended after H, the genesis key updated by the rotation walk to H inclusive. A sequence commit verifies under keyat(parent), so the rotation event verifies under the key it retires -- the naive 'in force at H' reading rejects exactly that one commit. A ledger entry verifies under keyat(base), and a rotation is the last admitted event of its batch, which puts the boundary between two entries and never inside one. R3 claimed rotation is already in-band kernel machinery; measured, it is not, so R3 says so and case 11's epoch block is marked blocked rather than counted as coverage.\n\nThe SHA-256 disagreement is resolved by measurement. Neither figure is this workroom's root: 366791a7 pairs the sha256 empty tree with sixty-four ASCII 1 bytes, 3eef49be pairs it with this workroom's 40-hex genesis, a hybrid describing no real workroom. Both reproduce twice, from a non-Git implementation and from git commit-tree in a fresh sha256 repository.\n\nThe consistency sweep found nine pairs, all mine, and two were design faults. A candidate carrying a parent was required to be a ledger parent and forbidden from being one; the side-parent bound wins, so it takes an unretained=parented line consuming no parent, and the cost is stated -- its reason is the drain's word until the submitter produces the candidate. And the release step said to record refusals into a ledger entry already written and pushed in the claim transaction, which cannot be done.","body":{"branch":"task/deployment-story","commit":"07b454ee2d6c061392f5f2cd6cfd72f968fc9f62","head":"07b454ee2d6c061392f5f2cd6cfd72f968fc9f62","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}